    "excerpt": "Below are several grant‑making programs and funding streams that commonly support the kind of cooperative‑building work you describe for the Austin Coop Business Foundation (ACBF).  Many of them are o",
    "content": "Below are several grant‑making programs and funding streams that commonly support the kind of cooperative‑building work you describe for the Austin Coop Business Foundation (ACBF).  Many of them are open to organizations in Austin and Central Texas and align with your focus on democratic‑owner‑controlled housing, marketplaces and workplaces.\n\n| Grant source | What it funds | Why it’s a fit for ACBF | How to apply / where to find details |\n|--------------|---------------|------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| **Sustainable Economies Law Center – Cooperatives Program** | Technical assistance, legal support, and seed‑grant funding for worker‑owned, resident‑owned, and community‑owned cooperatives. | The Center’s mission is exactly the one you describe – “building just and resilient economies through local ownership” and it already lists resources for worker cooperatives and worker‑self‑directed nonprofits【1】. | Visit the Cooperatives Program page on the Sustainable Economies Law Center website; sign up for their newsletter to receive alerts about upcoming grant cycles. |\n| **Business Cooperative Development Program (partnered with Denver Economic Development & Opportunity)** | Education, training, and startup grants for converting businesses to employee‑owned models. | Although the example program is based in Denver, the structure (partnering a city economic‑development office with a cooperative‑building organization) can be replicated in Austin. It demonstrates a model that city agencies (e.g., Austin Economic Development Department) can fund. | Use the program description as a template when approaching the City of Austin’s Economic Development & Small Business Division to propose a similar grant or pilot. |\n| **Texas Rural Cooperative Center (TRCC)** | Grants and technical assistance for rural and peri‑urban cooperatives, including housing co‑ops and food‑system co‑ops. | TRCC already partners with the Austin Cooperative Coaching and Training Program you listed, so they are a natural funding partner for scaling coaching, training, and the Community‑Owned Food Retail Initiative. | Check TRCC’s “Funding Opportunities” page; submit a project proposal that highlights your community‑impact goals. |\n| **City of Austin – Economic Development Department, Small Business Division** | City‑sponsored technical‑assistance contracts, matching‑grant programs, and “co‑op incubator” funds. | Your current Austin Cooperative Coaching and Training Program is already partnered with this division, making it a clear entry point for additional grant money. | Contact the Small Business Division’s grant manager; ask for the “Co‑operative Development Grant” application packet. |\n| **Austin Community Foundation (ACF) – Grants for Community‑Economic Development** | Multi‑year grants for projects that increase economic agency, affordable housing, and equitable wealth distribution. | ACF’s focus on “community‑led solutions” matches your goals of democratic skill‑building and cooperative ecosystem creation. | Review ACF’s grant guidelines on their website and submit a proposal that ties your programming to their impact metrics. |\n| **U.S. Department of Agriculture – Rural Development (Cooperative Development Grants)** | Grants for cooperative formation, capacity‑building, and infrastructure in rural and underserved areas of Texas. | Central Texas includes many rural communities that could benefit from housing and food‑retail co‑ops; USDA funds are often earmarked for exactly this type of work. | Apply through the USDA Rural Development portal; use the “Cooperative Development Grant” (CDG) application. |\n| **Texas Workforce Commission – Workforce Development Grants** | Funding for training, apprenticeship, and workforce‑development initiatives that increase local employment. | Your “democratic management” and “co‑op entrepreneurship” training aligns with workforce‑development objectives. | Search the TWC grant database for “Cooperative Workforce Development” and submit a project budget that includes your coaching services. |\n| **Community Development Block Grant (CDBG) – City of Austin** | Flexible funding for projects that benefit low‑ and moderate‑income residents, including affordable‑housing co‑ops. | Your Housing Committee’s work on resident‑owner assistance and land‑code reform can be framed as a CDBG‑eligible activity. | Coordinate with Austin’s Planning & Development Department to submit a CDBG application; reference your housing‑co‑op pilot. |\n| **Texas Enterprise Fund (TEF) – Innovation & Economic‑Growth Grants** | Large‑scale, performance‑based grants for projects that create jobs and diversify the state economy. | If you can demonstrate that a network of worker‑owned businesses will generate significant local employment, TEF may consider your ecosystem plan. | Prepare a business‑case study and submit through the Texas Economic Development & Tourism Office. |\n\n### How to move forward\n\n1. **Create a master grant calendar** that tracks application deadlines for the programs above (most are annual or semi‑annual).  \n2. **Leverage existing partnerships** (e.g., with the Texas Rural Cooperative Center, Austin Economic Development Department, and the Sustainable Economies Law Center) to co‑author proposals—joint applications often receive higher scores.  \n3. **Package your impact metrics**: number of co‑ops served, jobs created, affordable‑housing units secured, food‑access outcomes, and democratic‑skill training hours. Funding agencies look for clear, measurable results.  \n4. **Develop a “Co‑op Ecosystem Blueprint”** that outlines how each grant will fund a specific piece of your programming (e.g., coaching, legal services, advocacy, housing‑code reform). This narrative helps reviewers see the integrated nature of your work.  \n\nIf you need deeper details on any of these grant programs—such as specific eligibility criteria, budget templates, or contact persons—let me know and I can help you locate the exact application guides.\n",
