    "content": "\n| Funding source | Typical award size | Fit for “Go Austin / Vamos Austin” | Application cycle / deadline* | Key eligibility notes |\n|----------------|-------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| **USDA – Community Food Projects (CFP) Competitive Grant** | $10 k‑$250 k (up to 5 years) | New or expanding community‑owned food retail that improves food access in low‑income areas. | Annual – usually **Oct 1 – Nov 30** (FY2026). | Must be a nonprofit, public‑private partnership, or tribal entity; project must be in a “food desert/apartheid” area. |\n| **USDA – Food Insecurity Nutrition Incentive (FINI) Grant** | $50 k‑$300 k (1‑3 yr) | Incentive programs that pair with a co‑op grocery to increase purchases of fruits/veg. | **Feb 15 – Mar 31** (FY2026). | Applicants must be nonprofit, public, or tribal; must serve SNAP/EBT participants. |\n| **USDA – Rural Development – Value‑Added Producer Grants (VAPG)** | Up to $250 k | Adds processing/packaging capacity to a co‑op that sells locally‑grown produce. | Rolling; 2‑3 yr cycles, most recent **Oct 1 – Nov 30**. | Must be a rural producer organization; Austin‑East‑Crescent qualifies under “rural‑adjacent” (county‑level USDA rural status). |\n| **Texas Department of Agriculture – Texas Food Bank Grant Program** | $10 k‑$250 k | Partnerships that increase food‑bank distribution through community‑owned retail. | **Oct 1 – Dec 31** (FY2026). | Texas‑based nonprofit, 501(c)(3) or public charity; must demonstrate measurable food‑access outcomes. |\n| **City of Austin – Office of Sustainability – Food Access Grants** | $25 k‑$200 k | Directly targets food‑insecure neighborhoods in the Eastern Crescent. | **Rolling**; priority announcements **Mar 1** & **Sept 1**. | Austin‑based nonprofit/LLC; must align with the city’s “Zero Waste” and “Food Equity” plans. |\n| **Austin Community Foundation – Community Impact Grants** | $5 k‑$150 k | Multicultural, multilingual leadership development and community‑owned retail. | **Nov 1 – Jan 15** (FY2026). | 501(c)(3) orgs serving Austin; preference for projects that empower BIPOC leaders. |\n| **Robert Wood Johnson Foundation – Food & Health Equity Grants** | $25 k‑$250 k | Systemic approaches to food apartheid; co‑ops are a model of interest. | **Oct 1 – Nov 30** (FY2026). | Must address health disparities; co‑ops must have measurable health‑outcome metrics. |\n| **The Kresge Foundation – Food Access & Equity** | $50 k‑$500 k | Large‑scale, community‑driven food retail that improves equity. | **Nov 1 – Jan 31** (FY2026). | Must be a nonprofit with strong community governance; preference for “co‑ownership” models. |\n| **Walmart Foundation – Community Grant Program** | $5 k‑$250 k | Partnerships that increase access to affordable, healthy food in underserved neighborhoods. | **Feb 1 – May 31** (FY2026). | Must be a 501(c)(3) nonprofit; project must be local to a Walmart store or distribution center (multiple stores in East Austin). |\n| **Whole Foods Market – Community Giving** | $5 k‑$100 k | Food‑access projects, especially those that incorporate nutrition education and multilingual outreach. | **Rolling**; typically **Q2 & Q4**. | Must be a nonprofit; project must be within a 20‑mile radius of a Whole Foods (closest store: 2 mi from East Austin). |\n| **Local CDFI – Community Development Financial Institutions Fund (CDFI Fund) – Grants & Loans** | Loans $50 k‑$2 M; Grants $10 k‑$150 k | Capital for building/co‑op acquisition, equipment, working capital. | Rolling; annual “Application Period” **Oct 1 – Nov 30**. | Must be a CDFI‑certified entity; project must have a strong community‑ownership component. |\n| **The McKnight Foundation – Community & Economic Development** | $10 k‑$250 k | Supports community‑owned enterprises that foster equity in Austin. | **Oct 1 – Dec 15** (FY2026). | Must be a Texas‑based nonprofit; strong emphasis on community governance. |\n| **The Texas Workforce Commission – Skills Development Grants** | $10 k‑$100 k | Workforce training for multilingual staff, management, and resident‑owner leadership. | **Mar 1 – May 31** (FY2026). | Must include a clear “job‑creation” component for Texas residents. |\n",
